# Question: what does the work waiting in our queues (ready for development,
# for review, for QA, for release) cost in value not delivered, and how likely
# is it to exceed 1M euros a year?
#
# Run it (it uses the shared formulas):
#   ubc --scenario product/queue-cost
# Change an assumption, for example a WIP limit that keeps the queues short:
#   ubc --set 'queued = 4 to 8' --scenario product/queue-cost
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens yearly_cost --scenario product/queue-cost
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Amounts are in euros. Every
# item that waits loses its cost of delay for as long as it waits, so the
# queue costs the number of items in it times their weekly cost of delay
# (Reinertsen: queues are the biggest hidden cost in product development).
#
# Sources: Don Reinertsen, The Principles of Product Development Flow (2009),
# for the cost of queues, and John D. C. Little, "A Proof for the Queuing
# Formula L = λW" (1961), for Little's law; the ranges are typical experience.

# Assumptions
queued = 10 to 30                 # items waiting, not being worked on; count them on the board
cod_per_item = 500 to 3k          # average weekly cost of delay of one of them; typical experience
throughput = 4 to 7               # items the team finishes a week; read it from your tracker

# Calculation
wait = lead_time(queued, throughput)                   # weeks an item spends in the queues (Little's law)
weekly_cost = queued * cod_per_item                    # value lost every week the queues stay this long
yearly_cost = delay_cost(weekly_cost, weeks_per_year)  # the same over a year

# Result
wait
weekly_cost
yearly_cost
P(yearly_cost > 1M)               # how likely are the queues to cost more than 1M euros a year?

# Output (seed 1, 5000 samples):
#   wait = ≈ 3.26   90% [1.77 … 5.97]   98% [1.3 … 7.9]   ▃▅▇██▇▅▅▃▃▂▂▂▁▁▁
#   weekly_cost = ≈ 20.9k   90% [7490 … 59.1k]   98% [4700 … 93k]   ▅██▆▄▄▃▂▂▂▁▁▁▁▁▁
#   yearly_cost = ≈ 1.09M   90% [390k … 3.07M]   98% [240k … 4.8M]   ▅██▆▄▄▃▂▂▂▁▁▁▁▁▁
#   P(yearly_cost > 1M) = 0.56
